  "text": "The rain lashed against the windowpane, a rhythmic drumming that seemed to synchronize with the erratic beating of Elias’s heart. He sat in the dimly lit study, the only light source being the flickering glow of the old fireplace, casting long, dancing shadows across the walls. On the desk before him lay a single, yellowed photograph, its edges worn soft from years of anxious handling. It depicted a younger version of himself, standing beside a woman whose smile was as bright and fragile as the morning dew. They had been inseparable, bound by a love that felt eternal, until the day the silence fell. It was not a dramatic departure, but a slow, creeping withdrawal, a fading of presence that left behind only an echo. Now, years later, the house felt too large, the rooms too empty. The silence was not peaceful; it was heavy, pressing against his eardrums, demanding acknowledgment. He reached out, his fingers hovering over the image, afraid that touching it might shatter the last remaining connection to the past. The fire crackled, sending sparks into the dark, each one a tiny, fleeting hope. He wondered if time was a healer or merely a mask, hiding the wounds beneath a layer of dust and routine. Outside, the storm raged on, indifferent to his internal turmoil, while inside, the clock ticked with relentless precision, marking the passage of moments that could never be reclaimed. He closed his eyes, trying to remember the sound of her laughter, but it was already slipping away, dissolving into the rain, leaving him alone with the ghosts of what could have been."
